I hate to interrupt a good argument, but your calculations diverge
wildly from reality right here.


Many monsters can be generated with a potion of (full |extra |)healing
in a few ways, other than finding one on the ground or one being
created in a death drop:

Shopkeepers (75% have healing, 50% have extra healing as well)

Anything granted a random defensive item may, as its item, receive any
of the three potion types listed above.  These are generated with
equal probability.


Other sorts of potions may also be generated in monster initial
inventory in the following ways:

nymphs may have a potion of object detection (50%)

Pestilence always gets a potion of sickness, and any potion of full
healing generated in its inventory via rnd_defensive_item will instead
become a potion of sickness.

Anything with a random offensive item may receive a potion of: acid,
confusion, blindness, sleeping, paralysis.  These are generated with
equal probability.

Anything with a random misc item may receive a potion of polymorph,
speed, invisibility, or gain level.  These are not generated with
equal probability.


(Information from src/makemon.c and src/muse.c)

I expect (though I haven't estimated it yet) that the relative
frequencies of these potions being generated in this way differ from
their frequencies of normal generation.  Defensive items are more
frequent than offensive items are more frequent than misc items, btw.

